L151: # media-1
L152: Supplementary Figures.
L153: Figure S1. Maximal capacity test performance parameters.
L154: Pilot testing was conducted to determine time of day performance. Eighteen animals were split into two groups and subject to a maximal capacity test at either ZT13 or ZT22. The maximal test was conducted on a treadmill were the speed increased by 3 cm/s every 2 min until exhaustion. Animals were then randomly assigned to either a sedentary control group (n = 6), an exercise training group at ZT13 (n = 6), or an exercise training group at ZT22 (n = 6). Ten days after the pilot test, the first maximal test of the new groups was conducted. Each maximal test was conducted at ZT13 for the ZT13 exercise training group and at ZT22 for the ZT22 exercise training group. Mice were then subject to 6 weeks of endurance exercise training 5 times per week for 1 hour at 70 % of maximum at either ZT13 or ZT22. To determine maximal performance, the animals completed 3 maximal capacity tests, 1 at the beginning of week 1 before training began: 2 at the end of 3 weeks of training, and 3 at the end of week 6 of training. (A) Shows the distance in meters each group traveled in each of the maximal capacity tests. (B) Displays the time taken in minutes for each group to reach exhaustion for each of the maximal capacity tests. All data shown as MEAN ± SD with individual data points displayed as blue circles. Data for ZT13 exercise training group is shown as yellow bars and ZT22 exercise training group is shown as purple bars. Statistically significant differences are shown between groups *P<0.05, **P<0.01, ***P<0.001, ****P<0.0001 assessed by independent t-test; ns denotes no significant change.
L155: Figure S2. Individual Animal Performance Across 6 Weeks of Training
L156: This figure is presented to differentially visualize adaptations within individual mice. Mice were assigned to an exercise training group after pilot testing. Individual mouse trajectories are show for each group: ZT13 is shown in yellow (n = 6) (A), and ZT22 is shown in purple (n = 6) (B). Each maximal test was conducted at ZT13 for the ZT13 group and at ZT22 for the ZT22 group. Mice were subject to 6 weeks of endurance exercise training 5 times per week for 1 hour at 70 % of maximum at either ZT13 or ZT22. To determine maximal performance across the program, the animals completed 3 maximal capacity tests: first prior to the onset of training (Max 1); then, at the end of the third week of training (Max 2); and lastly, at the end of the sixth week of training (Max 3). Statistical results match those found in figure 1C from the main manuscript.
L157: Figure S3. Weekly cage activity profiles.
L158: Mice were individually housed and subject to 6 weeks of endurance exercise training 5 times per week for 1 hour at 70 % of maximum at either ZT13 or ZT22. Food consumption data is displayed for all animals in each group across 6 weeks of time-of-day exercise training for the sedentary control group shown in black (n = 6), ZT13 exercise training group shown in yellow  (n = 6), and ZT22 exercise training group shown on purple (n = 6). Data displayed as MEAN ± SD. Statistically significant differences are shown between groups *P<0.05, **P<0.01, ***P<0.001, ****P<0.0001 assessed by two-way ANOVA.
L159: Figure S4. Amplitude of PER2::LUC rhythms.
L160: Amplitudes of PER2::LUC rhythms were analysed by comparing average and peak bioluminescence values. In each graph black represents sedentary controls (CON), the ZT13 group is shown in yellow, and the ZT22 group is shown in purple. Extensor digitorum longus (EDL) (A), soleus (SOL) (B), lung (C), white adipose tissue (WAT) (D), suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) (E). Data displayed as MEDIAN with upper and lower quartiles. Statistically significant differences are shown between groups *P<0.05, **P<0.01, ***P<0.001, ****P<0.0001 assessed by one-way ANOVA; ns denotes no significant change.
L161: Figure S5. Effects of 6 weeks exercise training on the central circadian clock.
L162: Mice were subject to 6 weeks of endurance exercise training 5 times per week for 1 hour at 70 % of maximum at either ZT13 or ZT22. All animals in the sedentary control group (n = 2), ZT13 (n = 2) and ZT22 (n = 2) exercise training groups were killed 72 hours after their last bout of exercise, and tissues were placed in a lumicycle for culture at ZT17. All data is presented as MEAN ± SD unless otherwise stated and shows the sedentary control group always in black, the ZT13 exercise training group in light grey and the ZT22 exercise training group shown in darker grey, all for the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN). Real-time bioluminescent tracing (baseline subtracted) of PER2::LUC activity is shown over 3 consecutive days for the explanted SCN for the sedentary control, ZT13 (A), and ZT22 (B) groups. Triangles above A and B represent the relative peak of circadian phase. The average bioluminescent tracing (baseline subtracted) of PER2::LUC activity over 24 hours in explanted SCN, plotted relative to zeitgeber time for the control, ZT13 (C), and ZT22 (D) groups. The annotated exercise training line in C and D represent the relative time each group was subject to exercise training. Circadian phase is displayed in hours for the SCN (E) with differences assessed by one-way ANOVA. A phase plot labelled by zeitgeber time shows the relative peak of the average circadian phase for SCN (F) for all groups. Arrows show direction of phase change from the control group for each experimental group. The quantification of the exercise training-induced shift in circadian phase from sedentary control is shown for SCN (G). A decrease in phase indicates a phase advance, and an increase in circadian phase represents a phase delay. Absolute change in phase was compared between exercise training groups and control using independent t-test. All statistically significant differences are shown between groups *P<0.05, **P<0.01, ***P<0.001, ****P<0.0001; ns denotes no significant change.
[29ms]

